0065 | \begin{abstract}
0066 | 
0067 |     We study high dimensional expansion beyond simplicial complexes (posets) and focus on $q$-complexes which are complexes whose basic building blocks are linear spaces. We show that the complete $q$-complex (consists of all subspaces of a given linear space) may have non-trivial homology groups and therefore some techniques for simplicial complexes fail. We develop new techniques to work bypass this. In particular:
0068 | 
0069 |     \begin{enumerate}
0070 |         \item We describe a new construction of cones and use it to determine when the homology of the complete $q$-complex is trivial. We use this construction to prove the ''projective support dimension conjecture'' conjectured by Mnukhin and Siemons \cite{MNUKHIN}.
0071 | 
0072 |         \item We define topological high dimensional expansion for posets, and show that the complete $q$-complex has linear (in the number of lines) coboundary expansion.
0073 | 
0074 |         \item We define the $q$-Linial-Meshulam model of random $q$ complexes and prove a sharp threshold for the connectivity of random $q$-complexes.
0075 |     \end{enumerate}
0076 | \end{abstract}
0077 | 
0078 | 
0079 | \section{Introduction}
0080 | 
0081 | In recent years, a theory of ''high dimensional expanders'' has emerged, and been successfully applied in mathematics and computer science to resolve longstanding open problems (\cite{LTCPaper}, \cite{LogConcave}). Another thread of research is using the geometry of vector spaces to obtain better construction in TCS, most notably, in the seminal works  \cite{UGC3}, \cite{UGC2} a weaker variant of the unique games conjecture was reduced to an expansion problem on the Grassmann graph which was later solved in \cite{UGC1}. The Grassmann graph is a graph whose vertices are the $\ell$ dimensional linear spaces of some $\mathbb{F}_q^n$ and two subspaces $U, W$ are connected by an edge if $dim(U \cap W)=\ell-1$.
0082 | 
0083 | In this work we initiate the study of coboundary high-dimensional expansion on structures beyond simplicial complexes (in contrast to spectral expansion which was studied in \cite{KTTensors}), and mainly specialize in understanding $q$-complexes (see \Cref{sec : setting} for definition) which is a collection of linear subspaces of some space $V$ that are closed under inclusion.
0084 | This gives rise to a boundary map, for which the boundary of a subspace $U$ is the formal sum of all the subspaces of codimension $1$ contained in $U$.
0085 | 
0086 | 
0087 | \subsection{The setting}\label{sec : setting}
0088 | 
0089 | Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional vector space with $dim(V)=n$, over a finite field $
0090 | \mathbb{F}_q$. Denote by $Gr(V)$ the set of all subspaces of $V$ and by $Gr_k(V)$ the set of all $k$ dimensional subspaces of $V$. We also use the notation $Gr(n)$ to denote $Gr(\mathbb{F}_q^n)$. Similarly define $Gr_k(n)=Gr_k(\mathbb{F}_q^n)$.
0091 | 
0092 | We remind that $|Gr_k(n)|$ is the Gaussian binomial coefficient, denoted by ${n \choose k}_q$ and satisfies:
0093 | \begin{align*}
0094 |     |Gr_k(V)|={n \choose k}_q = \frac{(q^n-1) \ldots (q^n-q^{k-1})}{(q^k-1) \ldots (q^k-q^{k-1})}
0095 | \end{align*}
0096 | 
0097 | 
0098 | \begin{definition}
0099 |     A $q$-complex $X$ is a collection of \emph{subspaces} of some vector space $V$ closed under inclusion. That is: $ U \in X \implies Gr(U) \subseteq X$.
0100 | \end{definition}
0101 | 
0102 | This should be thought of as a $q$-analog of simplicial complexes where the ''$q=1$ limit'' is simplicial complexes. One defines analogously the set of $k$ faces, $X(k)$ to be the set of all $k$-spaces in $X$.
0103 | 
0104 | Fix a ring $R$. A formal sum $\Sigma_{U \in X(k)} \alpha_U U$ with $\alpha_U \in R$ is a $k$-chain of $X$ over $R$. The space of all $k$ chains is an $R$-module denoted by $C_k(X)$. One can define a boundary map $\partial_k : C_k(X) \rightarrow C_{k-1}(X)$ by extending linearly:
0105 | 
0106 | \[ \partial_k(U)= \Sigma_{W \subseteq_1 U} W  \]
0107 | 
0108 | Where $W \subseteq_1 U$ denotes that $W$ is a subspace of co-dimension $1$ in $U$.
0109 | 
0110 | This boundary map was previously considered in the paper \cite{MNUKHIN} by Mnukhin and Siemons, who studied the homology of the complete $q$-complex (collection of all subspaces of a given fixed space $V$).
0111 | 
0112 | The space of $k$ cochains is defined by $C^k(X) = Hom(C_k(X),R)$, and the a coboundary operator $d_k:C^k(X) \rightarrow C^{k+1}(X)$ is the adjoint operator of $\partial_{k+1}$, and satisfies:
0113 | 
0114 | \[ d_k(\alpha)(U)=\alpha(\partial_{k+1}(U))\]
0115 | 
0116 |  If the complex is finite one can abuse the notation and consider $d_k: C_k(X) \rightarrow C_{k+1}(X)$ by associating $U \leftrightarrow \delta_U$ where $\delta_U$ is the cochain that satisfies: $\delta_U(W)= \delta_{U=W}$. where the R.H.S is the Kronecker delta function. We also use $\mathbf{0}$ to denote the $0$-chain supported on the zero space with coefficient one, that is $\mathbf{0} = 1 \cdot Span\{0\}$
0117 | 
0118 | We say $t \in \mathbb{Z}$ is zero in the unital ring $R$ if $t$ maps to zero under the natural map $\mathbb{Z}\to R.$  When the ring $R$ is suitable the map $\partial$ is indeed a boundary map:
0119 | \begin{claim}\label{claim: delta squared}
0120 |     $\partial^2=0$ if and only if $ (q+1)r = 0$ for all $r\in R$.
0121 | \end{claim}
0122 | 
0123 | \begin{proof}
0124 |     Let $[\alpha]_W$ denote the $W$ coordinate with respect to the standard basis of $C_k(X)$ of $\alpha$. Then:
0125 | 
0126 |     \[[\partial^2(r \cdot U)]_W=\#\{W \subseteq_1 Y \subseteq_1 U\} \cdot r \]
0127 | 
0128 |     By the correspondence theorem, this set is in bijection with lines in the space $\faktor{U}{W} \cong \mathbb{F}_q^2$. As there are $q+1$ such lines:\[[\partial^2(r \cdot U)]_W = (q+1)r  \]
0129 | \end{proof}
0130 | 
0131 | \begin{remark}
0132 |     We use the notation $\partial^{(R)}$ to denote the operators $\partial$ when the chains and cochains are over a ring $R$ which may not satisfy $\left( \partial^{(R)}\right)^2=0$.
0133 | \end{remark}
0134 | 
0135 | We can define (co)boundaries and (co)cycle in an analogous way to the definition in the theory of simplicial complexes. Elements of $Z_k(X,R)= ker(\partial_k)$ are cycles, and elements of $Z^k(X,R)= ker(d_k)$ are cocycles. $B_k=Im(\partial_{k+1})$ is the set of $k$-boundaries  and $B^k=Im(d_{k-1})$ is the set of $k$-coboundries.
0136 | 
0137 | From now on, unless stated otherwise we assume $ (q+1)r= 0 $ for all $r \in R$. This means that $B_{k} \subseteq Z_k$ by \Cref{claim: delta squared} therefore we can define the homology group of $X$ by $H_k(X,R)=\faktor{Z_k}{B_{k}}$ and the cohomology group by $H^k(X,R)=\faktor{Z^k}{B^{k}}$.
0138 | 
0139 | We use the notation $C_\bullet(Gr(V))$ to denote the chain complex associated with $Gr(V)$ with the boundary map above.
0140 | 
0141 | \subsection{Homology of $C_\bullet(Gr(V))$}
0142 | 
0143 | In \Cref{sec : homological properties} we study the homological properties of the complete $q$-complex $C_\bullet(Gr(V))$ for some $V$. We give a different, elementary, proof to a result of \cite{MNUKHIN} about the homology of the complete $q$-complex:
0144 | 
0145 | \begin{theorem}\label{thm - homologies tot}
0146 |     Let $V$ be an $n$ dimensional space over $\mathbb{F}_q$ then:
0147 |     \begin{enumerate}
0148 |         \item If $n=2k+1$, then $C_\bullet(Gr(V))$ is exact. That is $H_t(Gr(V))=0$ for all $t$.
0149 | 
0150 |         \item If $n=2k$, then  $H_t(Gr(V))=0$ for all $t \neq k$.  That is all homology groups are trivial \emph{except the middle one}.
0151 |     \end{enumerate}
0152 | \end{theorem}
0153 | 
0154 | 
0155 | 
0156 | The same conclusion is true for the cohomology groups, as $H_t(Gr(V)) \cong H^{n-t}(Gr(V))$.
0157 | 
0158 | In \cite{MNUKHIN}, Mnukhin and Siemons asked for a different, direct, proof (''It would be desirable to have a direct proof of this result'').
0159 | A key ingredient in our proof is a new construction of topological cones for the Grassmannian. In particular, \cite{MNUKHIN} conjectured that $Ker(\partial^{(R)})$ is generated by rather compact elements, for all rings $R$ for which $char(R)$ is coprime to $q$, (they named this conjecture by ''Projective Support Dimension Conjecture''). Our construction confirms their conjecture:
0160 | 
0161 | 
0162 | \vspace*{10px}
0163 | \noindent\textbf{{Proposition  \ref{thm : small generators}.}}
0164 |    \emph{ $Ker(\partial^{(R)}_k)$ is generated by chains $\alpha_1,\ldots, \alpha_t$ such that each $\alpha_i$ is supported on a $2k$ dimensional subspace.
0165 | }
0166 | \vspace*{0.1px}
0167 | 
0168 | The techniques we develop are robust and suitable for studying the homology of other posets (in particular differential posets), as well as proving $Ker(\partial_k)$ has compact generators.
0169 | 
0170 | 
0171 | 
0172 | 
0173 | \subsection{Coboundary expansion}
0174 | 
0175 | Coboundary expansion was first defined in the foundational works of Linial, Meshulam and Wallach (\cite{LM}, \cite{MW}) on the $k$-homological connectivity of random complexes. It was later defined again in the pioneering work of Gromov on topological overlap \cite{Gromov}.
0176 | 
0177 | For a cochain $\alpha$ its size is defined by $|\alpha|=|\spp(\alpha)|$. Define the size of the cohomology class of $\alpha$ to be the minimum size over elements in the class of $\alpha$, that is
0178 | \[\norm{[\alpha]} = \min\{ |\alpha - d_{k-1}\beta| \text{ for } \beta \in C^{k-1}\}.\]
0179 | 
0180 | \noindent With those two notions, one can generalize the coboundary expansion of graphs to other cochain complexes. The coboundary expansion at dimension $k$, $h_k$ is defined by:
0181 | 
0182 | \[h_k = \min_{\alpha \in C^k(X) \setminus B^{k}(X)} \left\{ \frac{|d_k\alpha|}{\norm{[\alpha]}} \right\} \]
0183 | 
0184 | In \Cref{sec : expansion} we show (with explicit bound on $C_k$):
0185 | 
0186 | 
0187 | \vspace*{10px}
0188 | \noindent\textbf{{Theorem  \ref{thm : expansion}.}}
0189 |    \emph{ Let $h_{k,n}$ be the expansion constant of $k$ cochains of $Gr(V)$ where dim $V=n>2k$. There exist a constant $C_k$ that depends on $k,q$ such that:
0190 |     \[h_{k,n} \geq  C_k {n \choose 1}_q\]}
0191 | \vspace*{0.1px}
0192 | 
0193 | 
0194 | We remark that our proof also applies in the case $q=1$, though the bound on the expansion constant is noticeably worse than the optimal one obtained by Meshulam and Wallach \cite{MW}.
0195 | 
0196 | \subsection{Linial-Meshulam model for posets}
0197 | 
0198 | We consider the connectivity of Linial-Meshulam random $q$-complexes. Let $X \subseteq Gr(\mathbb{F}_q^n)$ be a $k+1$ dimensional complex with $X(k)=Gr_{k}(\mathbb{F}_q^n)$, the Linial-Meshulam random $q$-complex is the probability measure $\triangle^q(n,k,p)$ such that the probability of $X$ is:
0199 | \[\mathbb{P}[X]=p^{|X(k+1)|}\cdot(1-p)^{ {n \choose k+1}_q - |X(k+1)|}\]
0200 | 
0201 | A $q$-complex is $k$ connected if $H^k(X)=0$. We show that Linial-Meshulam random $q$ complexes exhibit phase transition at $p = \ln  {n \choose k}_q  / {n-k \choose 1}_q $.
0202 | 
0203 | 
0204 | \begin{theorem}\label{thm : qLMW}
0205 |     Let $X \sim \triangle^q(n,k,p)$ be a random $k$ dimensional $q$-complex. For any $\omega(n) \rightarrow \infty$:
0206 | 
0207 |     \begin{enumerate}
0208 |         \item If $p= \left( \ln \left( {n \choose k}_q \right) - \omega(n) \right)/ {n-k \choose 1}_q $ then $Pr[H^k(X) = 0] \rightarrow 0$.
0209 | 
0210 |         \item If $p=\left( \ln \left( {n \choose k}_q \right) + \omega(n) \right)/ {n-k \choose 1}_q $ then $Pr[H^k(X) = 0] \rightarrow 1$.
0211 |     \end{enumerate}
0212 | \end{theorem}
0213 | 
0214 | \subsection{General definitions for graded posets}
0215 | 
0216 |  Consider $(X, \leq,rk )$ a ranked poset. That is, $(X,\leq)$ is a poset with a rank function $rk:X\to \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}$ which satisfies (i) there is a unique element $0 \in X$ such that $rk(0)=0$, (ii) for $x < y$ one has $rk(x) < rk(y)$, and if there is no $z \in X$ such that $x < z < y$ then $rk(x)+1=rk(y)$. In such case one gets a natural partition of $X$ by $rk$ valuations $X=X(0) \cup X(1) \cup \ldots$, where $X(i)=\{x \in X \mid rk(x)=i\}$.
0217 | 
0218 | Define the space of chains $C_k(X)$, over $R$ to be the set of formal $R$ linear combinations of finitely many elements of $X(k)$, and the space of cochains as $C^k(X) = Hom(C_k(X), R)$. To have a notion of homology one needs a boundary signing map:
0219 | 
0220 | \[  [-,-] : X(k) \times X(k-1) \rightarrow R\]
0221 | 
0222 | If the boundary signing map is the constant $1$ function we say the boundary map is the modular boundary map. The boundary operator is defined by extending linearly:
0223 | 
0224 | \[ \partial (x) = \sum_{y \leq x , rk(y)=rk(x)-1} [x,y] y \]
0225 | 
0226 | This gives rise to the space of $k$-cycles $Z_k(X,R)=Ker(\partial_k)$, the space of $k$-boundaries $B_k(X,R)=Im(\partial_k)$, and when $\partial^2=0$ one can define the $k^{th}$ homology group of $X$ with $R$-coefficients by $H_k(X,R)=\faktor{Z_k(X,R)}{B_k(X,R)}$.
0227 | 
0228 | The space of cochains is defined naturally as $C^k(X) = Hom(C_k(X), R)$, and the coboundary operator is defined as being the adjoint of $\partial$, that is: $d_k \alpha (x) = \alpha(\partial_{k+1} x)$. The space of $k$-cocycles is $Z^k(X,R)=Ker(d_k)$, $k$-coboundaries $B^k(X,R)=Im(d_{k-1})$, and when $\partial^2=0$ one can define the $k^{th}$ cohomology of $X$ by $H^k(X,R)=\faktor{Z^k(X,R)}{B^{k-1}(X,R)}$.
0229 | 
0230 | For a cochain $\alpha$ define $|\alpha|=|\spp(\alpha)|$, the weight of it's cohomology class is \[\norm{[\alpha]} = \min\{ |\alpha - d_{k-1}\beta| \text{ for } \beta \in C^{k-1}\}.\]
0231 | \noindent The \emph{$k^{th}$ cosystolic expansion constant} of $X$ is defined by:
0232 | 
0233 | \[h_k = \min_{\alpha \in C^k(X) \setminus Z^{k}(X)} \left\{ \frac{|d_k\alpha|}{\norm{[\alpha]}}  \right\} \]
0234 | \begin{remark}
0235 | In applications posets may also be associated with a weight function, see e.g. \cite{KTGarland}. Such a weight function indces a norm and inner product on the spaces of chains and cochains, which generalize the uniform weight we have used above. One can similarly define expansions with respect to such weights.
0236 | \end{remark}
0237 | 
0238 | In the paper, we mainly focus on the complete Grassmannian complex which has almost all homologies trivial (\Cref{thm - homologies vanish middle}), and therefore coboundary expansion is equivalent to cosystolic expansion in almost all cases.
0239 | 
0240 | Finally, the \emph{Linial-Meshulam random $X-$subcomplex of rank $k$ with probability $p$}, denoted by $\triangle(X,k,p)$ is the probability measure such that the probability supported on subposets $X'$ of $X$ satisfying $\cup_{i=0}^{k-1} X(i) \subseteq X' \subseteq \cup_{i=0}^{k} X(i)$ such that the events $y \in X'$ are i.i.d $Bernulli(p)$ random variables.
