0246 | \section{Homological Properties of $C_\bullet(Gr(V))$}\label{sec : homological properties}
0247 | 
0248 | This section studies the homological properties of $C_\bullet(Gr(V))$ for some subspace $V$. We start with the following useful Heisenberg-like identity that relates linearly the operators $\partial_{k+1}d_k, d_{k-1}\partial_k, id$:
0249 | 
0250 | \begin{claim}\label{claim: clifford}
0251 |     If $n$ is odd then $\partial_{k+1}d_k - d_{k-1}\partial_k = (-1)^{k} id$
0252 | \end{claim}
0253 | 
0254 | \begin{proof}
0255 |     The term $d_{k-1}\partial_k (U)$ corresponds to \enquote{walking} down to a subspace of $U$ and then going up to a subspace containing it. The symmetric term corresponds to walking up and then down. Therefore if we look at the $U$ coordinate of L.H.S. $[d_{k-1}\partial_k(U)]_U = \qbin{k}{1}{q}$ since this corresponds to walking to a codimension $1$ subspaces of $U$ and returning to $U$. Similarly $[\partial_{k+1}d_k(U)]_U =\qbin{n-k}{1}{q}$ since this is the number of $W$ with $U \subseteq_1 W \subseteq V$.
0256 | 
0257 |     Notice that $\qbin{t}{1}{q}$ satisfies the following modulo $q+1$:
0258 | 
0259 |     \begin{align*}
0260 |         \qbin{t}{1}{q} = \sum_{i=0}^{t-1} q^i = \sum_{i=0}^{t-1} (-1)^i = \mathds{1}_{(t \mod 2)=1}
0261 |     \end{align*}
0262 | 
0263 |     Since $char(R) \mid q+1$ we get $\qbin{t}{1}{q} = (t \mod 2)$ in the ring $R$ as well. Therefore by the assumption that $n$ is odd:
0264 | 
0265 |     \begin{align*}
0266 |         [\partial_{k+1}d_k (U) - d_{k-1}\partial_k (U) ]_U= (n-k \mod 2) - (k \mod 2)  = (-1)^{k}
0267 |     \end{align*}
0268 | 
0269 |     For $W \neq U$ the walk $U \rightarrow U\cap W \rightarrow W$ exists if and only if the walk $U \rightarrow U + W \rightarrow W$ exists and these are the only possible walks, therefore, we get: $[\partial_{k+1}d_k (U) - d_{k-1}\partial_k (U)]_W=0$
0270 | 
0271 |     This concludes that $\partial_{k+1}d_k - d_{k-1}\partial_k = (-1)^{k} id$ for odd $n$.
0272 | \end{proof}
0273 | 
0274 | \begin{corollary}\label{coro : exactness for odd}
0275 | For odd $n$ the full Grassmannian (as a chain complex) is exact.
0276 | \end{corollary}
0277 | 
0278 | \begin{proof}
0279 |     If $\partial_k(x)=0$ then by plugging $x$ to the identity above $x = \partial \left( (-1)^{k} d_k(x) \right)$.
0280 | \end{proof}
0281 | 
0282 | \subsection{Constructing cones}
0283 | 
0284 | A cone $c$ is a family of linear homomorphisms from $C_k(Gr(V))$ to $C_{k+1}(Gr(V))$ (they depend on $k$ which will usually be clear from context and therefore omitted) that satisfies the identity:
0285 | 
0286 | \begin{align}\label{eq - cone identity}
0287 |     \partial c(\alpha) = \alpha - c(\partial \alpha)
0288 | \end{align}
0289 | 
0290 | The name cone comes from the construction of a cone over a topological space that satisfies the identity above. We often have a few families of cones that depend on some index $b$, in this case, we will denote the cone with index $b$ of the chain $\alpha$ by $c_{b,\alpha}$.
0291 | 
0292 | In this subsection, we construct cones by using the \Cref{claim: clifford}. The main idea of our construction is to use the identity locally (while restricting it to some small subspace) instead of the whole space.
0293 | 
0294 | Let $\mathcal{B}$ be the set of all ordered bases of $V$. We will construct for each $(b_1,...,b_{n})= \vec{b} \in \mathcal{B}$ and $u \in C_k(X)$ a $k+1$ chain $c_{\vec{b},u}$ that satisfy $\partial c_{\vec{b},u} = u - c_{\vec{b},\partial(u)}$. For a $k$ dimensional subspace $W$ define the subspace $W_{\vec{b}}^{(i)}$ to be the subspace $W + Span \{ b_1, ..., b_i \}$. Let $i^*$ be the first index such that $dim
0295 | \left(W_{\vec{b}}^{(i^*)} \right)= 2k+1$, define $W_{\vec{b}}:= W_{\vec{b}}^{(i^*)} $.
0296 | 
0297 | \begin{lemma}\label{lem : defin}
0298 |     Let $\vec{b}$ be a basis of $V$, and let $W \leq V$ be a subspace of  $V$ of dimension $k$. Then:
0299 | 
0300 |     \begin{enumerate}
0301 |         \item $W \leq W_{\vec{b}}$
0302 | 
0303 |         \item If $ W' \leq W$ then $W'_{\vec{b}}\leq W_{\vec{b}}$
0304 |     \end{enumerate}
0305 | \end{lemma}
0306 | 
0307 | \begin{proof}
0308 | The first bullet is clear by definition of $W_{\vec{b}}$ as the first subspace of dimension $2k+1$ containing $W$ by appending vectors from $\vec{b}$.
0309 | 
0310 | For the second bullet, assume $dim(W')=t<k$, then by definition $W'_{\vec{b}} = W'+Span\{b_1,\ldots,b_r\}$ and $r$ is the minimal $r$ such that $dim(W'_{\vec{b}} )=2t+1$. Since $W'$ is of co-dimension $k-t$ in $W$ it implies that $dim(W+Span\{b_1,\ldots,b_r\})\leq 2t+1 +(k-t)=k+t+1 <2k+1$, therefore $W+Span\{b_1,\ldots,b_r\}\leq W_{\Vec{b}}$. This concludes the proof as:
0311 | 
0312 | \[ W'_{\vec{b}} \leq W+Span\{b_1,\ldots,b_r\} \leq  W_{\Vec{b}}\]
0313 | 
0314 | \end{proof}
0315 | 
0316 | \begin{definition}
0317 |     The cone $c_{\vec{b},\bullet}$  w.r.t. $\vec{b}$ is defined recursively on generators $W\in Gr(V),$ and then extended linearly to $C_\bullet(Gr(V)).$
0318 |     This is done as follows. We set $c_{\vec{b}, \mathbf{0} }= span \{ b_1 \}$ and for $W\in Gr_k(V)$ we define:
0319 | \begin{align*}
0320 |     c_{\vec{b},W} =  \left.d_k\right|_{W_{\vec{b}}} \left(  (-1)^{k} \left(W - c_{\vec{b},\partial(W)} \right) \right)
0321 | \end{align*}
0322 | 
0323 | \end{definition}
0324 | 
0325 | \begin{remark}
0326 |     A crucial remark is that by \Cref{lem : defin} the definition above is well defined. A priori,  $c_{\vec{b},\partial W}$ may be supported on a big subspace of $V$. By \Cref{lem : defin} it follows that assuming the support of $c_{\vec{b},W'}$ is contained in $W'_{\vec{b}} \leq W_{\vec{b}}$ for $W' < W$, it follows that the entire chain $W - c_{\vec{b},\partial(W)}$ is contained in $W_{\vec{b}}$ and thus one can indeed restrict the coboundary operator to the subcomplex contained in $W_{\vec{b}}$. Since $c_{\vec{b}, \mathbf{0} }= span \{ b_1 \}$ it follows by induction that $\spp(c_{\vec{b},W})$ is contained in $W_{\vec{b}}$ as needed.
