0337 | \begin{proposition}\label{Proposition - Cone Identity}
0338 |     For $u\in C_k (Gr(V))$ we have $\partial c_{\vec{b},u} =  u - c_{\vec{b},\partial(u)}$
0339 | \end{proposition}
0340 | 
0341 | \begin{proof}
0342 |     It is enough to prove the identity for a $k$ space $W$. The proof is by induction on $k$. Indeed, by definition, we have:
0343 | 
0344 |     \begin{align*}
0345 |         \partial (c_{\vec{b},W})  = \partial \left(  \left.d_k\right|_{W_{\vec{b}}} (-1)^{k} \left(W - c_{\vec{b},\partial(W)}  \right) \right)
0346 |     \end{align*}
0347 | 
0348 | Since $dim (W_{\vec{b}})$ is odd we can apply \Cref{claim: clifford} and get that:
0349 | 
0350 |     \begin{align*}
0351 |         \partial \left(  \left.d_k\right|_{W_{\vec{b}}}  (-1)^{k} \left(W - c_{\vec{b},\partial(W)}  \right)\right) &=   \left.d_{k-1}\right|_{W_{\vec{b}}} \partial_k  \left(  (-1)^{k} \left(W - c_{\vec{b},\partial(W)}  \right) \right) + (-1)^k \left( (-1)^{k} \left(W - c_{\vec{b},\partial(W)}  \right) \right) \\
0352 |         &= \left.d_{k-1}\right|_{W_{\vec{b}}} \partial_k  \left(  (-1)^{k} \left(W - c_{\vec{b},\partial(W)}  \right) \right) +  \left(W - c_{\vec{b},\partial(W)}  \right)
0353 |     \end{align*}
0354 | 
0355 |     To prove the claim it's enough to show that
0356 |     \begin{align*}
0357 |         \left.d_{k-1}\right|_{W_{\vec{b}}} \partial_k  \left(  (-1)^{k} \left(W - c_{\vec{b},\partial(W)}  \right) \right) =0
0358 |     \end{align*}
0359 | 
0360 | Indeed, by induction $ \partial \left( c_{\vec{b},\partial(W)} \right) = \partial(W) - c_{\vec{b},\partial^2(W)}= \partial(W)$ :
0361 | 
0362 | \begin{align*}
0363 |     \partial_k  \left(  (-1)^{k} \left(W - c_{\vec{b},\partial(W)} \right) \right) &=  (-1)^{k} \partial (W) + (-1)^{k+1} \partial \left( c_{\vec{b},\partial(W)} \right) \\
0364 |     &= (-1)^{k} \partial (W) + (-1)^{k+1} \partial (W)  = 0
0365 | \end{align*}
0366 | 
0367 | \end{proof}
