0384 | We also note that in the space $Gr(\mathbb{F}_q^n)$ there is a projective duality between $k$ spaces and $n-k$ spaces that lifts to a duality between $k$-chains and $n-k$-cochains. Concretely, fix any non-degenerate bilinear form $\langle -,- \rangle$ on $\mathbb{F}_q^{n}$, e.g.  $\langle x,y \rangle =  \sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i \cdot  y_i$ and for a subspace $U$ define $U^{\perp} = \{v \in \mathbb{F}_q^{n} \mid \forall u \in U, \langle v,u \rangle = 0 \}$. We can extend $^{\perp}$ linearly to get an operation $^{\perp} : C_k \rightarrow C_{n-k}$.
0385 | 
0386 | \begin{claim}\label{Claim - Bilinear form duality}
0387 |     $\forall x \in C_k(\mathbb{F}_q^{n+1}): (\partial_{n-k}x)^{\perp} = d_{k}(x^{\perp}) $
0388 | \end{claim}
0389 | 
0390 | \begin{proof}
0391 |     It is enough to prove this for $x=U$ for some subspace $U$. For a subspace $U$ the claim follows since $U' \subseteq_1 U \iff U^{\perp} \subseteq_1 (U')^{\perp}$ for any non degenerate form.
0392 | \end{proof}
