0394 | \begin{corollary}\label{cor : middle symmetry}
0395 |     Let $V$ be an $n$ dimensional space. Then $H_t(Gr(V)) \cong H^{n-t}(Gr(V))$
0396 | \end{corollary}
0397 | 
0398 | We can use \Cref{Proposition - Cone Identity} to determine when the homology groups $H_t(Gr(V))$ vanishes when $dim(V)$ id even:
0399 | 
0400 | \begin{theorem}\label{thm - homologies vanish middle}
0401 | 
0402 | 
0403 | 
0404 |     Let $n=2k$, and $V$ be a linear space over $\mathbb{F}_q$ of dimension $n$. Then $H_t(Gr(V))=0$ for $t \neq k$.
0405 | \end{theorem}
0406 | 
0407 | \begin{proof}
0408 |     Fix some basis $\vec{b}$. Notice that if $\tau \in Z_t(X)$, i.e. $\partial \tau = 0$, and $t<k$ then $c_{\vec{b},\tau}$ is defined. By \Cref{Proposition - Cone Identity}:
0409 | 
0410 |     \begin{align*}
0411 |     \partial c_{\vec{b},\tau} = \tau - c_{\vec{b},\partial \tau} = \tau + c_{\vec{b},0} = \tau
0412 |     \end{align*}
0413 | 
0414 |     Therefore $\tau \in B_{t+1}(X)$ as needed. By the universal coefficient theorem for cohomology we also have that $H^t(X)$ is trivial. Finally, the groups $H_t, H^t$ are trivial for $t>k$ by \Cref{cor : middle symmetry}.
