0566 | \subsection{Elements in $H_n(Gr(\mathbb{F}_q^{2n}))$}
0567 | 
0568 | 
0569 | We now present two constructions of chains in different, non-trivial, homology classes of $H_n(Gr(\mathbb{F}_q^{2n}))$ denoted by $\eta_n,\psi_n$. These constructions appear in the work of James \cite{JamesRepTheory}, in his study of the representation theory of $GL(V)$, though we present them from the viewpoint of cones which in some cases is more general.
0570 | 
0571 | 
0572 | 
0573 | \subsubsection{Construction of $\eta_n$}
0574 | 
0575 | The following observation is immediate from the cone identity and gives an easy way to construct closed chains from existing ones.
0576 | 
0577 | \begin{observation}\label{obs - two cones}
0578 |     Let $\vec{b}_1,\vec{b}_2$ be bases then for $\alpha \in Z_k$ we have $c_{\vec{b}_1, \alpha}-c_{\vec{b}_2, \alpha} \in Z_{k+1}$.
0579 | \end{observation}
0580 | 
0581 | We now describe James's construction which may be viewed as successive uses of \Cref{obs - two cones}. Consider the independent sets  $\vec{b}^{+}_{2i} = (e_1, \ldots, e_{2i-2}, e_{2i-1})$, and $\vec{b}^{-}_{2i} = (e_1, \ldots, e_{2i-2}, e_{2i})$. The chains will be defined by: $\eta_0 = \mathbf{0}$ and $\eta_i = c_{\vec{b}^{+}_{2i}, \eta_{i-1}}- c_{\vec{b}^{-}_{2i}, \eta_{i-1}}$.
0582 | 
0583 | It is clear by the observation that $\eta_i \in Z_i(Gr(2i))$. One can also write an explicit formula for $\eta_n$. For any sequence of $(\epsilon_i)_{i=1}^n \in \{0,1\}^{n}$ and $A=\{a_{i,j}\}_{ 1 \leq i \leq j < n}$ with $a_{i,j} \in \mathbb{F}_q$ there will be a vector space in the support of $\eta_n$. Explicitly, define the following vector:
0584 | \begin{align*}
0585 |     v^{(\epsilon, A)}_i:= \sum_{j=1}^{i-1} a_{i,j} e_{2j-\epsilon_j} + e_{2i-1} + \epsilon_{i} \left( e_{2i}-e_{2i-1} \right)
0586 | \end{align*}
0587 | 
0588 | Then the spaces in the support will be:
0589 | 
0590 | \begin{align*}
0591 |     W^{(\epsilon,A)} := Span \{ v^{(\epsilon, A)}_1, \ldots, v^{(\epsilon, A)}_n \}
0592 | \end{align*}
0593 | 
0594 | Denote $sgn(\epsilon) = (-1)^{\sum \epsilon_i}$, then:
0595 | 
0596 | \begin{claim}[James \cite{JamesRepTheory}]\label{claim explicit form of two cones}
0597 |     $\eta_n = (-1)^{ { n \choose 2}} \sum_{\epsilon \in \{0,1\}^{n},A} sgn(\epsilon) W^{(\epsilon,A)}$ , where the sum is over $A$ as above.
0598 | \end{claim}
0599 | 
0600 | \begin{proof}
0601 |     The proof is by induction. For $n=1$ we have $c_{\vec{b}^{+}_{2}, \eta_{0}}=c_{e_1,0}= span\{e_1\}$, and similarily $c_{\vec{b}^{-}_{2}, \eta_{0}}= Span\{e_2\}$ thus by definition $\eta_1= Span\{e_1\} - Span\{e_2\}$.
0602 | 
0603 |     For $n+1$, we have by definition that $c_{\vec{b}^{+}_{2i}, \eta_{n}} = (-1)^n\restr{d \eta_{n}}{Span{\vec{b}^{+}_{2i}}}$. We can decompose $c_{\vec{b}^{+}_{2i}, \eta_{n}} = \beta^+ + \gamma^+$ where $\beta^+$ is supported on subspaces contained in $Span \{ e_1,\ldots, e_{2n} \}$ and $\gamma^+$ support is not contained in $Span \{ e_1,\ldots, e_{2n} \}$.
0604 | 
0605 |     We can decompose analogously $c_{\vec{b}^{-}_{2i}, \eta_{n}} = \beta^- + \gamma^-$. We know that $\beta^+=\beta^- = \restr{d \eta_{n}}{Span\{e_1, \ldots, e_{2n}\}}$. We conclude that:
0606 | 
0607 |     \begin{align*}
0608 |         \eta_{n+1} = (-1)^n \left(\gamma^+ - \gamma^-\right)
0609 |     \end{align*}
0610 | 
0611 |     It is clear that $\gamma^+, \gamma^-$ has disjoint support, since each subspace of $\gamma^+$ contains vector with a non zero $e_{2n+2}$ coordinate.
0612 | 
0613 |     We claim that each subspace $W^{(\epsilon,A)}$ in $\spp(\eta_n)$ of can be extended into $Span\{ e_1, \ldots, e_{2n}, e_{2n+2}\}$ in exactly $q^n$ ways by having a vector $e_{2n+2}+\sum_{j=1}^n a_{n,j} e_{2j-\epsilon_j}$. This follows since in the RREF (reduced row echelon form) of $W$ with respect to the standard basis $e_{2j-(1-\epsilon_j)}$ is a leading term of the matrix. Since subspaces with $e_{2n+2}$ come from the term $\gamma^{-}$, and correspond to $\epsilon_{n+1}=1$ then assuming $W^{(\epsilon,A)}$ has coefficient $sgn(\epsilon)$ then the sign of  $W^{((\epsilon,\epsilon_{n+1}),A)}$ is $-sgn(\epsilon) = sgn((\epsilon,\epsilon_{n+1}))$.
0614 | \end{proof}
0615 | 
0616 | \begin{remark}\label{remark - two cones cross polytopes}
0617 |     The cross polytope is the simplicial complex on the set $CP_n=\{s_1,s_2, \ldots, s_{2n} \}$ that consists of all subsets $S \subseteq CP_n$ for which $\forall i : |S \cap \{s_i,s_{n+i}\}| \leq 1$. In the cross polytope the elements $s_i, s_{n+i}$ are considered antipodal and usually given negatives signs in $\pm 1$. By definition, one can construct $CP_n$ by taking two cones of $CP_{n-1}$ with the two new antipodal vertices, $\{s_n, s_{2n}\}$.
0618 |     With this in mind, notice that the chain above is the analog of this decomposition of $CP_n$, and by setting $q=1$ there are no $A$ coefficients and the chain reduces to the signed chain of all $n$ sets of the $n$-cross polytope.
0619 | \end{remark}
