0621 | \subsubsection{Construction of $\psi_n$}
0622 | 
0623 | We now consider a different $q$-analog of the cross polytope, denoted by $\psi_n$. The construction is based on the theory of polar spaces and specifically, the polar space associated with the orthogonal quadratic form. We will review the facts we need from the theory of quadratic forms over a finite field to construct $\psi_n$ (we refer the reader to  \cite{Aschbacher}, \cite{Chevalley} for a thorough treatment)
0624 | 
0625 | Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional vector space over $\mathbb{F}_q$. Let $Q: V \rightarrow \mathbb{F}_q $ be a quadratic form, that is $Q$ satisfies $Q(av)=a^2Q(v)$ and also $B(u,v)=Q(u+v)-Q(u)-Q(v)$ is a billinear form. We assume $B$ is non-degenerate. In the cases $char(\mathbb{F}_q)\neq 2$ one can work with a symmetric bilinear form directly, but for $char(\mathbb{F}_q) = 2$ the extra information of $Q$ is necessary. A vector $v$ is \emph{singular} if $Q(v)=0$. A pair of singular vectors $\{e,f\}$ is a \emph{hyperbolic pair}, if $B(e,f)=1$.
0626 | 
0627 | \begin{fact}\label{decomposition to hyperbolic pairs}
0628 |     Every $V$ of dimension $2n$ as above has a decomposition $V=Span \{e_1,f_1, \ldots ,e_n,f_n \} $ where:
0629 | 
0630 |     \begin{enumerate}
0631 |         \item $e_i,f_i$ form an hyperbolic pair.
0632 | 
0633 |         \item $Span \{e_i,f_i\}$ is orthogonal to $Span \{ e_j,f_j \}_{j\neq i}$.
0634 |     \end{enumerate}
0635 | \end{fact}
0636 | 
0637 | \begin{definition}
0638 |     A subspace $W \subseteq V$ is totally singular if:
0639 | 
0640 |      \begin{itemize}
0641 |          \item $\forall w \in W: Q(w)=0$
0642 | 
0643 |          \item $\forall w_1,w_2 \in W: B(w_1,w_2)=0  $
0644 |      \end{itemize}
0645 | \end{definition}
0646 | 
0647 | A subspace $W \subseteq V$ is maximal totally singular if it is of dimension $n$. Let $MTS=MTS(V)$ be the collection of maximal totally singular spaces of $V$. The chain $\psi_n$ will have $MTS$ as support. Regarding signs we will need the following property which appears in \cite{Aschbacher}:
